---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Magellanic Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Magellanic Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Spheniscus magellanicus</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 61-76 cm (24-30 inches) tall, weighing 2.7-6.5 kg (6-14.3 lbs).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Breeds in coastal Argentina, Chile, and the Falkland Islands. Some migrate north to the waters off Brazil during the non-breeding season.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Near Threatened</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The Magellanic Penguin feeds on small fish, squid, and crustaceans, which they hunt in the coastal waters of the South Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Magellanic Penguins breed in large colonies, often nesting in burrows they dig themselves or in natural crevices. They form monogamous pairs that typically return to the same nest site each year. They are known for their loud, braying calls, which help them recognize their mates and chicks. Both parents participate in incubating the eggs and raising the young.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They have a distinctive black and white plumage with two black bands across their chest.</li>
                        <li>Magellanic Penguins can travel long distances during their migrations.</li>
                        <li>Chicks form crèches for protection when they are older.</li>
                        <li>They face threats from oil pollution, overfishing, and habitat degradation.</li>
                        <li>Their strong pair bonds and site fidelity are important aspects of their breeding biology.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>